# io.github.lucarickli.Platform//45: the runtime the apps in apps/ with a
# Containerfile run on, Fedora's own packages for what GUI apps need (GTK 3
# and 4, fonts, Mesa, the audio and D-Bus clients, Python) and for what its
# apps link or run that Fedora has (Remmina's RDP, VNC and SPICE, the Craft
# apps' lp). build/flatpak.sh makes the Flatpak runtime of the image of
# the flatpak stage: its /usr becomes the runtime's files, its /etc their
# etc; its labels, at the end of the stage, have the rest (ID, branch,
# environment, GL extension point). The test stage, last, is an app on the
# runtime whose command is the test (test.sh). x86_64 only.
#
# The apps build FROM this image too and keep only their /app
# (apps/org.mozilla.firefox/Containerfile). So dnf and rpm stay, in the image
# and with it in the runtime, where they and the rpm database cost some 35
# MB: an app's build stage installs its build tools with them, and the scan
# before the push (.github/actions/scan-image) asks this image's dnf which
# Fedora security updates it lacks, against the record written below. A
# separate sdk stage without them would be a second image for every build
# and scan to keep apart, to save 5% of the runtime.
#
# Fedora's release is the runtime's branch. This FROM, flatpak.branch below,
# and the flatpak.runtime of the test stage and of each app move together, in
# Renovate's Fedora PR, twice a year (the test fails while the first two
# differ); Fedora's updates come with every build.
FROM registry.fedoraproject.org/fedora:45 AS flatpak

# Fedora's updates first: the base image is days old, and dnf leaves a
# package it finds installed at that version. English only: the base
# image's %_install_langs (en_US) has rpm install a package's English files
# (%lang(en), every English locale's) and leave out every other language's,
# translations among them. The base image's own packages come with every
# language's, so those that have another's are reinstalled under it too.
# Then, without weak dependencies, each package below for what it is:
# - GTK 3 and 4, libadwaita, and what they load at run time that is only a
#   weak dependency, or none: librsvg2 (SVG icons), glib-networking (TLS for
#   GIO), gvfs-client (the host's gvfs, to an app that may talk to
#   org.gtk.vfs.*), at-spi2-atk (accessibility, GTK 3), cups-libs (GTK's
#   CUPS print backend, for an app with the cups socket), util-linux
#   (prlimit, which the base image's util-linux-core lacks: in a Flatpak,
#   gdk-pixbuf's loaders, glycin's, run in a sandbox of their own through
#   flatpak-spawn, under prlimit; without it no SVG loads, Adwaita's icons
#   among them, and GTK 3 aborts on the icon it draws for a missing one; the
#   test loads one);
# - fonts: Fedora's defaults (Noto Sans, Serif and Mono, Noto Color Emoji,
#   Cantarell), and Liberation, metric-compatible with the Arial, Times New
#   Roman and Courier New that web pages and documents ask for. No CJK (30
#   MB): the host's fonts are there as well (fontconfig, below). The
#   Adwaita icons and cursors;
# - Mesa's GL, EGL and GBM, its VA-API drivers (in mesa-dri-drivers), libva,
#   libglvnd and the Vulkan loader. Not Mesa's Vulkan drivers (180 MB):
#   NVIDIA's comes with its GL extension (the extension point, below);
# - sound: ALSA, routed to PulseAudio (alsa-plugins-pulseaudio: in the
#   sandbox there is the pulseaudio socket, no sound device), the PipeWire
#   and PulseAudio clients; speech-dispatcher's;
# - libnotify, libsecret, the CA certificates, D-Bus, Python 3 (an app's
#   helpers: Firefox's Pywalfox host), diffutils (cmp, for those in sh),
#   hunspell's English dictionary (en_US), glibc's English locales;
# - flatpak-xdg-utils: xdg-open and xdg-email through the portal (below);
# - Remmina's (apps/org.remmina.Remmina), what it and its plugins link:
#   FreeRDP 3 (freerdp-libs, libwinpr: RDP), libvncserver (its libvncclient:
#   VNC), vte291 (the terminal of SSH), spice-gtk3 (SPICE), libsodium,
#   Ayatana's AppIndicator (the tray icon), avahi-ui-gtk3 (hosts on the
#   LAN); and what it loads by name: pcsc-lite-libs (smart cards, which
#   FreeRDP loads to redirect them) and gstreamer1-plugins-good (14 MB, for
#   SPICE's sound alone: spice-gtk plays and records through its
#   autoaudiosink and autoaudiosrc; a SPICE session is silent without it);
# - the Craft apps' (apps/ai.storyteller.*): cups-client, the lp, lpstat and
#   lpoptions that PdfCraft, WordCraft and GridCraft run to print, through
#   the host's CUPS (the cups socket); libxkbcommon-x11, which winit loads for
#   the keyboard on X11, where there is no Wayland (all four).
# Last, from the same metadata (-C), the record of every security advisory
# dnf knew for these packages, which the scan before the push goes by, as
# for the dev container. The metadata comes first, three tries 30 s apart,
# as every download here is retried (dnf retries the packages itself).
RUN for i in 1 2 3; do dnf -q makecache && break; [ "$i" != 3 ] || exit 1; sleep 30; done \
    && dnf -y upgrade \
    && dnf -y reinstall $(rpm -qa --qf '%{NAME}[ %{FILELANGS}]\n' \
        | awk '{ for (i = 2; i <= NF; i++) if ($i !~ /^en/) { print $1; break } }') \
    && dnf -y --setopt=install_weak_deps=False install \
        gtk3 gtk4 libadwaita librsvg2 glib-networking gvfs-client at-spi2-atk cups-libs util-linux \
        fontconfig default-fonts-core-sans default-fonts-core-serif default-fonts-core-mono \
        default-fonts-core-emoji liberation-sans-fonts liberation-serif-fonts liberation-mono-fonts \
        adwaita-icon-theme adwaita-cursor-theme \
        mesa-dri-drivers mesa-libEGL mesa-libGL mesa-libgbm libglvnd-glx libva vulkan-loader \
        alsa-lib alsa-plugins-pulseaudio pipewire-libs pulseaudio-libs speech-dispatcher-libs \
        libnotify libsecret ca-certificates dbus-libs python3 diffutils hunspell-en-US \
        glibc-langpack-en flatpak-xdg-utils \
        freerdp-libs libwinpr libvncserver vte291 spice-gtk3 libsodium libayatana-appindicator-gtk3 \
        avahi-ui-gtk3 pcsc-lite-libs gstreamer1-plugins-good \
        cups-client libxkbcommon-x11 \
    && install -d /usr/share/fedora-bootc \
    && dnf -q -C advisory list --security --all --repo=updates --json \
        > /usr/share/fedora-bootc/advisories-at-build.json \
    && dnf clean all

# Fontconfig's configuration for a Flatpak runtime, as Fedora's own runtimes
# have it: fontconfig/ is Fedora's flatpak-runtime-config (dist-git
# rpms/flatpak-runtime-config, branch f44, d947a4c), whose package Fedora's
# repositories do not carry. It adds the app's fonts and the host's, which
# flatpak binds under /run/host, with the host's caches, and salts the
# runtime's font dirs so that their caches are told apart from the host's
# for the same paths.
COPY fontconfig/ /etc/fonts/conf.d/

# What makes it a Flatpak runtime rather than a system:
# - xdg-open and xdg-email are flatpak-xdg-utils', which hand the URI or the
#   mail to the host through the portal, as Fedora's own runtime names them.
# - The font caches, built for the dirs as flatpak deploys them: ostree
#   checks every directory out with mtime 0, and fontconfig takes a cache
#   for a dir with another mtime as stale, so each app would build its own
#   on its first start (the test checks it builds none). They go to
#   /usr/cache/fontconfig, the first cache dir 50-flatpak.conf names; the
#   fontconfig package's own, unsalted, go.
# - GL: the GL extension point's directory (its label, below), and glvnd's and
#   the Vulkan loader's /etc dirs pointing into it, where flatpak merges the
#   driver's vendor and ICD files, as org.freedesktop.Platform has them.
# - ld.so: an empty /etc/ld.so.conf, so that flatpak writes its own (the
#   app's /app/lib, and what the app's and the runtime's extensions, the GL
#   driver among them, add), as org.freedesktop.Platform has it; with any
#   other, flatpak's ld.so cache lacks the extensions' libraries, and
#   NVIDIA's GL driver does not load (flatpak.sh build checks it). Fedora's
#   includes ld.so.conf.d, so that must be empty: what a package put there
#   would be dropped.
# - Nothing of a system's: no systemd units or presets, no build-id links of
#   debug info that is not there, no /var (flatpak gives each app its own).
RUN ln -s flatpak-xdg-open /usr/bin/xdg-open \
    && ln -s flatpak-xdg-email /usr/bin/xdg-email \
    && rm -rf /usr/lib/fontconfig/cache /var/cache/fontconfig \
    && mkdir -p /usr/cache/fontconfig \
    && find /usr/share/fonts -type d -exec touch -d @0 {} + \
    && fc-cache -s -f \
    && mkdir -p /usr/lib64/GL \
    && rm -rf /etc/glvnd && ln -s ../lib64/GL/glvnd /etc/glvnd \
    && rm -rf /etc/vulkan && ln -s ../lib64/GL/vulkan /etc/vulkan \
    && test -z "$(ls -A /etc/ld.so.conf.d)" && : >/etc/ld.so.conf && ldconfig \
    && rm -rf /usr/lib/systemd/system /usr/lib/systemd/user /usr/lib/systemd/*-preset \
        /usr/lib/.build-id /var

# --- what the image does not say: the flatpak.* labels ----------------------
# The runtime's ID, and its branch, the image's Fedora release (the FROM
# above).
LABEL flatpak.id=io.github.lucarickli.Platform \
      flatpak.branch=45

# The environment Flathub's runtimes set (org.freedesktop.Platform's
# metadata), for what an app puts in /app, which here comes from Fedora
# packages too: libraries and modules in lib64, not lib. flatpak's own ld.so
# cache has /app/lib only (ld.so, above), hence LD_LIBRARY_PATH.
# XDG_DATA_DIRS ends in the host's share dirs, which flatpak binds in part
# (its icons, for their themes); XCURSOR_PATH does the same for the cursors
# that GTK 3 and Xcursor load themselves, the host's theme first.
# GST_PLUGIN_SYSTEM_PATH replaces GStreamer's default, so it names it too.
# The last two are the GL extension's (below).
LABEL flatpak.finish-args.env="\
    --env=PATH=/app/bin:/usr/bin \
    --env=LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/app/lib64 \
    --env=XDG_DATA_DIRS=/app/share:/usr/share:/run/host/user-share:/run/host/share \
    --env=GI_TYPELIB_PATH=/app/lib64/girepository-1.0 \
    --env=GST_PLUGIN_SYSTEM_PATH=/app/lib64/gstreamer-1.0:/usr/lib64/gstreamer-1.0 \
    --env=XCURSOR_PATH=/run/host/user-share/icons:/run/host/share/icons:/usr/share/icons \
    --env=__EGL_EXTERNAL_PLATFORM_CONFIG_DIRS=/etc/egl/egl_external_platform.d:/usr/lib64/GL/egl/egl_external_platform.d:/usr/share/egl/egl_external_platform.d \
    --env=GBM_BACKENDS_PATH=/usr/lib64/GL/lib/gbm:/usr/lib64/gbm"

# The GL drivers as Flathub ships them, for its 1.4 branch, which follows the
# host's driver: flatpak installs the one active-gl-driver names (NVIDIA's,
# org.freedesktop.Platform.GL.nvidia-<version>, matches the host's kernel
# module) into lib64/GL/<name>, puts its lib on the ld.so path and merges the
# subdirectories below into lib64/GL, where /etc/glvnd and /etc/vulkan point
# (above). Without one, as on Mesa's GPUs, Mesa in the runtime serves. The
# extension point is one --extension option for each key of its group in the
# metadata, with org.freedesktop.Platform's values, for lib64, less vdpau
# (no libvdpau here). Two merged dirs need the runtime's environment, as
# their users here are Fedora's builds, which look elsewhere:
# egl/egl_external_platform.d holds NVIDIA's EGL platforms for Wayland, xcb
# and GBM, which its EGL lacks without __EGL_EXTERNAL_PLATFORM_CONFIG_DIRS
# (and with it apps on Wayland fall back to software); lib/gbm holds its GBM
# backend, which libgbm takes for NVIDIA's device only from
# GBM_BACKENDS_PATH, Mesa's own (/usr/lib64/gbm) after it.
LABEL flatpak.finish-args.gl="\
    --extension=org.freedesktop.Platform.GL=version=1.4 \
    --extension=org.freedesktop.Platform.GL=directory=lib64/GL \
    --extension=org.freedesktop.Platform.GL=subdirectories=true \
    --extension=org.freedesktop.Platform.GL=add-ld-path=lib \
    --extension=org.freedesktop.Platform.GL=merge-dirs=vulkan/icd.d;glvnd/egl_vendor.d;egl/egl_external_platform.d;OpenCL/vendors;lib/dri;lib/d3d;lib/gbm;vulkan/explicit_layer.d;vulkan/implicit_layer.d \
    --extension=org.freedesktop.Platform.GL=download-if=active-gl-driver \
    --extension=org.freedesktop.Platform.GL=enable-if=active-gl-driver \
    --extension=org.freedesktop.Platform.GL=autoprune-unless=active-gl-driver \
    --extension=org.freedesktop.Platform.GL=no-autodownload=true \
    --extension=org.freedesktop.Platform.GL=autodelete=false"

# --- the test: an app of its own on the runtime, FROM scratch so that none
# of the labels above apply to it, whose command is test.sh. flatpak.sh test
# runs it in its sandbox, on the runtime as every app sees it ---------------
FROM scratch AS test
COPY --chmod=0755 test.sh /app/bin/platform-test
LABEL flatpak.id=io.github.lucarickli.Platform.Test \
      flatpak.runtime=io.github.lucarickli.Platform//45 \
      flatpak.command=platform-test
